ed a part  in transport canada's latest update 86  of flights left the country's top four  airports within an hour of their  scheduled departure time in early august  that is up from 75 percent the first  week of july still shy of the 92 percent  pre-pandemic it's marginal progress  demand typically drops in the fall as  people return to school and work  aviation analysts see an opportunity for  people looking for deals thanks to the  emergence of low-cost carriers like lynx  and flair airlines there's a lot more  competition for your travel dollars in  the fall than it would be in the summer  uh and that that competition translates  into lower fares so you'll see a  significant drop  in terms of pricing  on canadian routes but deals may not be  as easy to find for travel packages  abroad continuing the trend of higher  prices this summer tour operators are  dealing with inflated costs for just  about everything it's the combination of  hotels with  staffing they've had to put maybe wages  up of course airline tickets have gone  up as well  until airports and airlines improve  working conditions and adjust  compensation chronic staff shortages  will continue to be the new normal we're  going to be in those situations where  peak demand whether it's summer whether  it's christmas whether it's thanksgiving  whatever it is easter  you know you're all going to be  stressing the airports and the airlines  buckle up for more travel chaos on the  horizon ann gaviola global news toronto  stick around next indigenous pride faces  off with one of hollywood's most iconic  sci-fi monsters  by now either you or someone you know  has probably seen the sci-fi monster  movie pray the blockbuster has been the  number one streaming film in canada for  the past three weeks shot on alberta's  stoney nakota first nation the film is  the latest entry in the long-running  predator franchise but it's this film's  indigenous cast and the female lead  that's really turning heads as heather  